# Affine-space tutorial

"""
Linear interpolation of two points `p` and `q`
with a weight scalar `t`:
"""
p : Float[Unit[s] & Point[Time]] = 10.0
q : Float[Unit[s] & Point[Time]] = 4.0
"""The scalar t=0.25 has weight 1/4 tracked in its type"""
t : Float[Unit[1] & Affine[1, 1/4]] = 0.25
"""
Affine[1, 1/4]: 1.0 - t is a scalar of weight 3/4,
and the weights of the two terms sum to 1, hence we get
back a point from the linear interpolation.
"""
r : Float[Unit[s] & Point[Time]] = (1.0 - t) * p + t * q
it1 = r

"""However, we may not be able to statically resolve the
parameter, and so in this case we can fall back to the Scalar
construction, where Scalar : AffineSpace"""
"""
A Scalar is a scalar number of unknown value. It can scale a
Vector (weight 0), so linear interpolation p + t * (q - p)
works for any t, including a parameter whose value is not
known statically.
"""
def lerp (p, q, t) -> Float[Unit[s] & Point[Time]]:
  p : Float[Unit[s] & Point[Time]]
  q : Float[Unit[s] & Point[Time]]
  t : Float[Scalar]
  return p + t * (q - p)

a : Float[Unit[s] & Point[Time]] = 10.0
b : Float[Unit[s] & Point[Time]] = 4.0
it2 = lerp(a, b, 0.25)

# The two results are the same here, but may not be for all
# floating points
it = (it1, it2)
